{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to the EU AI Act, how are 'serious incident' and 'systemic risk' defined, and how do these definitions relate to fundamental rights?", "answer": "The EU AI Act defines 'serious incident' to include infringements of fundamental rights, and 'systemic risk' to cover negative effects on fundamental rights and society, integrating rights at the definitional level.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "the definition of a 'serious incident' under Article 3, point (49) of the AI Act includes not only physical harm or disruption to infrastructure, but also incidents that result in 'the infringement of obligations under Union law intended to protect fundamental rights.'"}, {"page": 3, "quote": "the definition of 'systemic risk' in Article 3, point (65), extends the notion of risk to include negative effects on fundamental rights and society as a whole, particularly from general-purpose AI models with high-impact capabilities."}]},
  {"question": "What procedural safeguards does the EU AI Act require for law enforcement use of real-time remote biometric identification, and what assessment must be conducted before use?", "answer": "The Act requires a fundamental rights impact assessment and prior registration in the EU database before law enforcement can use real-time remote biometric identification systems.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "the use of such systems by law enforcement authorities is conditioned on a fundamental rights impact assessment (FRIA) under Article 27 and prior registration in the EU database (Article 49)."}]},
  {"question": "What is the main compliance obligation for high-risk AI systems under Article 9 of the EU AI Act, and what types of risks must be addressed?", "answer": "High-risk AI systems must have risk management systems that address risks to fundamental rights, health, and safety throughout the AI system's life cycle.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "Such systems, grounded in Article 9, must encompass risks to fundamental rights, alongside risks to health and safety. This is another example of the AI Act's rights-based approach."}]},
  {"question": "According to the EU AI Act, which agency is a permanent member of the Advisory Forum, and what is its role in AI governance?", "answer": "The EU Agency for Fundamental Rights (FRA) is a permanent member of the Advisory Forum, ensuring expert input on fundamental rights in AI governance.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "The EU Agency for Fundamental Rights (FRA) has been designated as a permanent member of the Advisory Forum, which guarantees a continuous and expert voice on fundamental rights in the discussions surrounding AI governance."}]} ,
  {"question": "Which national authorities must be designated by EU Member States under the AI Act, and what expertise must they possess?", "answer": "Member States must designate national competent authorities with expertise in AI technologies, personal data protection, and fundamental rights.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "Article 70(3) of the AI Act explicitly integrates fundamental rights protection into this institutional design; it requires that the designated authorities possess expertise not only in AI technologies, but also in personal data protection and fundamental rights."}]},
  {"question": "What is the purpose of the transparency requirements for high-risk AI systems in the EU AI Act, and how do they support individuals' rights?", "answer": "Transparency requirements ensure deployers can understand AI outputs, supporting rights to good administration, effective remedy, and fair trial by enabling individuals to contest AI-based decisions.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "transparency obligations under Article 13 serve as a safeguard for the right to good administration and the right to an effective remedy and to a fair trial (Articles 41 and 47 of the Charter)."}]}
]}
